    <UnitTestResult executionId="9ec060f5-5de4-4802-a457-8a92a0f1af2b" testId="8849059d-94b6-ace8-0675-b6f66f788684" testName="WebApi.Tests.Meals.MealsInvitationIdentityBindingTests.Only_the_account_the_company_meant_ends_up_holding_the_membership" computerName="Svens-MacBook-Pro" duration="00:00:00.0010000" startTime="2026-08-05T00:30:07.0600960+02:00" endTime="2026-08-05T00:30:07.0600960+02:00" testType="13cdc9d9-ddb5-4fa4-a97d-d965ccfc6d4b" outcome="NotExecuted" testListId="8c84fa94-04c1-424b-9868-57a2d4851a1d" relativeResultsDirectory="9ec060f5-5de4-4802-a457-8a92a0f1af2b">
      <Output>
        <StdOut>OPEN RULING — Meals owner. A shared mailbox can claim another employee's invitation because the confirmed-email match does not require the address to identify ONE account (Identity's RequireUniqueEmail is unset and AspNetUsers.Email carries no unique index). Four remedies are written up — enforce address uniqueness, bind the invitation to an account id at issue, refuse an address resolving to more than one account, or require a second channel — and choosing between them changes what a company admin must type, so it is not a test's call. This fact states the property every remedy must satisfy and is unskipped the day one lands; A_shared_mailbox_colleague_can_still_take_the_membership_the_company_meant_for_someone_else fails on that same day and is what makes this skip impossible to forget.</StdOut>
      </Output>
    </UnitTestResult>
